# TODO — Notification Service

Day-sized milestones, in build order. Each one ends in something you can
actually run and see. Source of truth: `docs/dd.md`.

## Done
- [x] Solution layout: `src/`, `tests/`, `docs/`
- [x] Domain model: `Notification` + `NotificationStatus` with state transitions
- [x] Persistence: EF Core (PostgreSQL) `AppDbContext` + initial migration
- [x] Application interfaces: `INotificationService`, `INotificationRepository`,
      `IRepository`, `IUnitOfWork`, `IQueue`
- [x] `NotificationService` (create/get/update/delete)
- [x] Infrastructure: repositories, `UnitOfWork`, `InMemoryQueue`, DI wiring
- [x] Central error handling: `GlobalExceptionHandler` + ProblemDetails + HTTP logging
- [x] Automatic migrations on startup (Development)
- [x] M2 delivery pipeline: provider boundary, hosted worker, persisted
      `Queued → Processing → Sent`, duplicate-safe, structured lifecycle logs
- [x] M3 local infrastructure: Docker Compose, PostgreSQL, configuration-driven
      connections, automatic migrations
- [x] M4 RabbitMQ messaging: durable id-only messages, explicit acks,
      at-least-once delivery, reconnect-safe topology
- [x] M5 retries: exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s), persisted failure state,
      TTL retry queue, dead-letter queue for exhausted notifications
- [x] M6 test suite: unit tests + Testcontainers integration tests covering the
      design document scenarios

---

## M2 — Delivery pipeline, happy path ✅
Make queued work actually get delivered, behind a provider boundary.

- [x] `INotificationProvider` abstraction + fake email provider (can simulate failure)
- [x] Hosted background worker that consumes the queue
- [x] `Queued → Processing → Sent` transitions; skip notifications already `Sent`
- [x] Structured logging for queued / processing / delivered

**Done when:** after posting, the worker delivers it and the status becomes
`Sent`; re-delivering an already-`Sent` notification is a no-op.

Verified: `POST` → `Processing` → `Sent` with `ProcessedAt` set; replaying an
already-`Sent` id logs `already sent` and does not deliver again. Failure
simulation (`fail@example.com`) records `Failed`, `RetryCount`, and `LastError`.
The worker owns state transitions and persistence; the provider only delivers.
Queue messages carry the notification id only. Worker/provider live under
`Infrastructure/Processing` and `Infrastructure/Providers`.

---

## M3 — Local infrastructure & configuration ✅
Make the system runnable together with its real dependencies.

- [x] `docker-compose.yml` with SQL DB and RabbitMQ
- [x] DB and RabbitMQ connection details from configuration, not hardcoded

**Done when:** from a clean checkout, `docker compose up` starts the
dependencies and the app connects to them.

Verified: `docker compose up -d` starts PostgreSQL 17 and RabbitMQ 4
(management UI on `15672`). The API applies its migration on startup and stores
notifications in PostgreSQL using the `ConnectionStrings:DefaultConnection`
configuration value.

---

## M4 — Durable messaging with RabbitMQ ✅
Replace the in-memory queue with the real message boundary.

- [x] RabbitMQ-backed `IQueue<Notification>`, publishing only the notification id
- [x] Exchange + queue setup; explicit ack only after successful processing
- [x] At-least-once delivery; consumer tolerates duplicates

**Done when:** messages flow through RabbitMQ; killing the worker mid-flight and
restarting it still processes the pending work.

Verified: `POST` publishes a persistent, id-only message to the durable
`notifications` exchange/queue. A worker killed while a notification was
`Processing` left one message in RabbitMQ; restarting the worker picked it up,
delivered it and acknowledged it. The API publishes through
`Infrastructure/Messaging/RabbitMqQueue`, which declares the main queue and the
dead-letter exchange/queue. Ack happens only after the worker has persisted the
outcome; an unacknowledged channel close requeues the message.

---

## M5 — Retries & dead-lettering ✅
Handle the failure path described in dd.md.

- [x] Exponential backoff retries (1s, 2s, 4s …) with `RetryCount` tracked
- [x] Dead-letter queue for notifications that exhaust retries
- [x] Failure recorded on the notification; duplicate-safe retry handling

**Done when:** forcing provider failure shows retries then dead-lettering, and
the database reflects `Failed`.

Verified: posting to `fail@example.com` produced attempts at 1s, 2s and 4s. The
notification ended as `Failed`, `RetryCount = 4`, `LastError = "Simulated
provider failure"`, and one message in `notifications.dlq`. Failed retries are
published to a TTL retry queue that dead-letters back to the main queue.
Already-sent and already-exhausted notifications are acknowledged without a new
delivery attempt.

---

## M6 — Test suite & DoD verification ✅
Lock in the behaviour and confirm the end-to-end flow.

- [x] Test project(s) under `tests/` added to the solution
- [x] Cover the dd.md §13 scenarios: accepted/stored, published, delivered, retry on
  failure, retry exhaustion → DLQ, already-sent not re-delivered, status retrievable
- [x] Verify the full happy and failure paths end to end

**Done when:** `dotnet test` is green and both `Queued → Processing → Sent` and
`Queued → Processing → Failed → Retry → Failed/DLQ` are demonstrated.

Verified: `dotnet test` passes 15 unit tests and 5 integration tests. Integration
tests use Testcontainers to start PostgreSQL 17 and RabbitMQ 4, run the real API
host and worker, and assert the HTTP status, persisted state, retry count, DLQ
message and duplicate-delivery behaviour. Unit tests cover the domain state
transitions, `NotificationService` persistence/publishing and the in-memory
queue.

